## Mitigation Patterns

[List common security controls or defensive patterns in your codebase that Hound should verify are present and correctly implemented.]

Common security patterns to verify:

[For ZK-SNARK circuits:]
- Constraint completeness checks (all signals constrained)
- Range checks for field elements
- Input validation constraints (public inputs, witness bounds)
- Non-zero enforcement for denominators
- Boolean constraints for binary signals
- Uniqueness constraints for identifiers

[For ECDSA/Cryptography:]
- Constant-time operations (no data-dependent branches/memory access)
- Nonce generation from secure CSPRNG
- Point validation (on-curve checks, subgroup checks)
- Scalar validation (range checks, non-zero checks)
- Proper error propagation (no silent failures)
- Key zeroization after use

[For blockchain nodes:]
- Block validation checks (PoW/PoS, signatures, state root)
- Transaction validation (nonce, gas, signature)
- State transition verification
- Resource limits (gas, block size, memory)
- Reorg protection (finality rules)
- P2P rate limiting and peer scoring

[For smart contracts:]
- Reentrancy guards (nonReentrant modifier, checks-effects-interactions)
- Access control modifiers (onlyOwner, onlyRole)
- Input validation (require statements, bounds checks)
- Safe arithmetic (SafeMath, checked operations)
- Pull payment pattern (withdraw instead of send)
- Time-delay or multi-sig for admin operations

[For crypto wallets:]
- Secure key storage (encryption at rest, hardware isolation)
- BIP32/BIP44 derivation standards
- Transaction validation (amount, recipient, gas, chain ID)
- Signature verification before broadcast
- Replay protection (chain ID, nonce tracking)
- Mnemonic entropy requirements (128-256 bits)

[**Tip:** Include library-specific patterns (e.g., circomlib gadgets, OpenZeppelin contracts, libsecp256k1 functions, consensus-specs helpers).]

