## External Dependencies Examples

[List common third-party libraries, frameworks, or standard libraries that should be EXCLUDED from detailed node creation because they are well-known and trusted.]

Common external libraries/frameworks to exclude from node creation:

[For ZK-SNARK circuits:]
- circomlib (standard circuit library)
- snarkjs (proof generation and verification)
- bellman (zk-SNARK library)
- arkworks (cryptography library)
- halo2 (zkSNARK proving system)

[For ECDSA/Cryptography:]
- libsecp256k1 (ECDSA library)
- OpenSSL/BoringSSL (crypto primitives)
- ring (Rust crypto library)
- noble-curves (JS cryptography)
- GMP/MPFR (arbitrary precision math)
- BearSSL (embedded crypto)

[For blockchain nodes:]
- Go-Ethereum/Geth (Ethereum client)
- Prysm/Lighthouse/Teku (consensus clients)
- libp2p (p2p networking)
- LevelDB/RocksDB (database)
- Prometheus (metrics)
- consensus-specs (Ethereum spec)

[For smart contracts:]
- OpenZeppelin (security library)
- Chainlink (oracle framework)
- SafeMath (arithmetic library - pre-Solidity 0.8)
- UUPS/Transparent proxies (upgrade patterns)
- ds-test/forge-std (testing frameworks)

[For crypto wallets:]
- ethers.js/web3.js (Ethereum libraries)
- bitcoinjs-lib (Bitcoin library)
- bip39 (mnemonic library)
- hdkey (BIP32 implementation)
- scrypt/argon2 (KDF libraries)
- Trezor/Ledger SDKs (hardware wallet interfaces)

[**Tip:** Include well-audited, standard crypto/blockchain libraries. This helps Hound focus on YOUR implementation logic, not battle-tested dependencies. However, DO analyze how you USE these libraries (parameter validation, error handling, etc.).]

