## Additional Notes

[Optional section: Add any other project-specific guidance, domain knowledge, or special considerations that might help Hound analyze your codebase more effectively.]

[Examples for crypto/blockchain projects:]

[For ZK-SNARK circuits:]
- Trusted setup ceremony details (if applicable)
- Field modulus and curve parameters in use
- Known soundness assumptions (e.g., "relies on discrete log assumption over BN254")
- Performance-critical constraint count targets
- Compatibility requirements (e.g., "must work with Groth16 backend")

[For ECDSA/Cryptography:]
- Curves in use (secp256k1, secp256r1, ed25519, BLS12-381, etc.)
- Constant-time requirements for all secret-dependent operations
- Side-channel threat model (timing, power, cache, fault injection)
- Compliance standards (FIPS 140-2, Common Criteria, etc.)
- Known cryptographic assumptions (e.g., "relies on hardness of ECDLP")

[For blockchain nodes:]
- Consensus mechanism (PoW, PoS, BFT, etc.)
- Fork choice rule and finality guarantees
- Known consensus attacks to defend against (long-range, nothing-at-stake, etc.)
- Performance targets (TPS, block time, state size)
- Network upgrade/hard fork handling

[For smart contracts:]
- Upgrade patterns in use (transparent proxy, UUPS, beacon, etc.)
- Oracle dependencies and trust assumptions
- Cross-chain bridge security requirements
- MEV protection strategies
- Invariants that must NEVER be violated (e.g., "total supply <= max cap")

[For crypto wallets:]
- Supported blockchains and derivation paths
- Hardware wallet integration requirements
- Secure enclave / SGX / TEE usage
- Multi-signature or threshold signature schemes
- Compliance requirements (KYC, AML, sanctions screening)
- Recovery mechanisms (social recovery, seed phrases, etc.)]

